{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/b1-explainer-about-chapulines-anticuchos-natto-stinky-tofu-mopane-worms-and-kitfo-six-part/audio/main-afad7414698c.mp3","durationSec":363.816,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.44,"text":"The first surprising dish comes from Latin America."},{"start":3.72,"end":8.5,"text":"Chapulines are grasshoppers that people commonly eat in parts of Mexico and Central America."},{"start":9.4,"end":12.32,"text":"The name comes from Nahuatl, an old language of the region."},{"start":13.48,"end":22.74,"text":"Cooks toast the grasshoppers and season them with garlic, lime juice, chilies, or salt, so the final snack is crunchy, salty, and a little sour."},{"start":23.74,"end":31.58,"text":"It helps to think about every dish in three simple steps: where the food comes from, how people change it, and what the result feels like."},{"start":32.4,"end":38.24,"text":"For chapulines, the source is an insect, the change is toasting with seasoning, and the result is a snack."},{"start":39.3,"end":44.9,"text":"People eat them alone or as a filling, for example inside a tlayuda, a large tortilla dish."},{"start":46.06,"end":54.36,"text":"Oaxaca is the main region for chapulines, and they are also popular near Mexico City, Puebla, Cuernavaca, and Tepoztlán."},{"start":54.36,"end":62,"text":"This is not a new fashion: Spanish records from the early to mid-16th century already mention grasshoppers being eaten."},{"start":62.82,"end":71.72,"text":"Anticuchos are popular and inexpensive meat skewers from the Andes mountains, especially Peru, and you can also find them in Bolivia, Chile, and Ecuador."},{"start":72.6,"end":75.14,"text":"The surprise here is not the cooking but the cut of meat."},{"start":75.92,"end":80.16,"text":"The most popular version, anticuchos de corazon, uses beef heart."},{"start":81.08,"end":86.66,"text":"Cooks marinate the meat in vinegar and spices such as cumin, aji pepper, and garlic, then grill it."},{"start":87.04,"end":89.34,"text":"A boiled potato often sits at the end of the skewer."},{"start":90.32,"end":98.12,"text":"Using the same three steps, the source is an organ cut, the change is marinating and grilling, and the result is a smoky street food."},{"start":98.52,"end":108.24,"text":"The history explains the choice of meat: in colonial times, Spanish households valued organ cuts less, so beef heart became common in this cheap street food."},{"start":109.16,"end":114.26,"text":"Today, people across Peru eat anticuchos, rich and poor alike."},{"start":115.4,"end":119.58,"text":"A part of the animal that some people throw away is here the star of the dish."},{"start":121.32,"end":122.8,"text":"Now the story moves to Asia."},{"start":123.68,"end":131.68,"text":"Natto is a traditional Japanese food made from whole soybeans fermented with a bacterium called Bacillus subtilis var."},{"start":131.68,"end":132.4,"text":"natto."},{"start":133.44,"end":140.16,"text":"Many people in Japan eat it for breakfast on top of rice, often mixed with mustard, soy sauce, and spring onion."},{"start":140.9,"end":152.94,"text":"It is most popular in eastern Japan, in regions such as Kanto, Tohoku, and Hokkaido, so someone from Tokyo may love it while a visitor from another country stares at the bowl."},{"start":153.36,"end":165.52,"text":"The source is an ordinary bean, but the change, fermentation, produces a very unusual result: a strong smell, a sticky, slimy texture with long strings, and a strong flavor."},{"start":166.36,"end":171.62,"text":"Many people call natto an acquired taste, which means you learn to like it over time."},{"start":172.32,"end":176.74,"text":"Still, a 2009 survey found that about 70 percent of respondents liked the taste."},{"start":177.52,"end":182.1,"text":"For a large part of Japan, this strange-looking food is simply a normal morning."},{"start":183.18,"end":188.8,"text":"Stinky tofu is a Chinese fermented tofu dish, and its name says everything about the first impression."},{"start":189.54,"end":195.92,"text":"It is famous for a very strong smell, which people sometimes compare to strong cheese or even garbage."},{"start":196.52,"end":201.12,"text":"Yet the taste is often much milder and savory than the smell suggests."},{"start":202.04,"end":206.98,"text":"Stinky tofu is a common night-market snack in Taiwan and is also eaten in many parts of China."},{"start":206.98,"end":214.98,"text":"It can be fried, steamed, stewed, or grilled, and fried stinky tofu is especially common at night markets."},{"start":216.62,"end":218.84,"text":"Here the three steps show an interesting gap."},{"start":219.48,"end":227.52,"text":"The source is soft tofu, the change is fermentation, and the result is a food whose smell and taste seem to belong to two different dishes."},{"start":228.56,"end":236.36,"text":"A legend links an early version to a man named Wang Zhihe in the Qing dynasty, whose leftover tofu fermented in a jar."},{"start":236.98,"end":241.98,"text":"Fans go further than accepting the smell: they say that smellier batches can actually taste better."},{"start":242.92,"end":244.98,"text":"The last two dishes come from Africa."},{"start":245.82,"end":254.7,"text":"Mopane worms are not really worms but large edible caterpillars of the moth Gonimbrasia belina, common in the warmer parts of southern Africa."},{"start":255.52,"end":259.2,"text":"They feed mainly on the leaves of the mopane tree, which gives them their name."},{"start":260.12,"end":266.3,"text":"In the region they are an important source of protein, so for many families this is serious food, not a dare."},{"start":267.2,"end":269.16,"text":"The source is a caterpillar from the trees."},{"start":269.94,"end":276.34,"text":"The change starts with cleaning, then people often boil the caterpillars with salt and dry or smoke them."},{"start":276.9,"end":287,"text":"The result can be eaten in two ways: dried mopane worms make a crunchy snack, or cooks stew them with onion, tomato, and spices for a warm meal."},{"start":287.42,"end":294.46,"text":"The dried caterpillars keep well, which is one reason they are traded in countries such as Botswana, Namibia, South Africa, and Zimbabwe."},{"start":295.4,"end":300.18,"text":"Like chapulines in Mexico, an insect here becomes both an everyday snack and a full dish."},{"start":302.24,"end":307.04,"text":"Kitfo is an Ethiopian dish of minced raw beef from the tradition of the Gurage people."},{"start":307.58,"end":311.5,"text":"The surprise for many visitors is that the beef is raw or almost raw."},{"start":312.28,"end":322.36,"text":"The meat is mixed with mitmita, a hot chili spice powder, and niter kibbeh, a spiced clarified butter, so the flavor is rich and spicy rather than plain."},{"start":323.22,"end":331.19,"text":"When the beef is cooked lightly rare instead of fully raw, the dish is called kitfo leb leb."},{"start":331.19,"end":341.67,"text":"Following the three steps one last time, the source is fresh beef, the change is mincing and seasoning without real heat, and the result is a soft, warm, buttery dish."},{"start":342.27,"end":353.17,"text":"Kitfo is often served with ayibe, a mild cheese, or gomen, cooked greens, and it comes with injera, a soft flatbread, or with kocho among Gurage cooks."},{"start":353.81,"end":358.25,"text":"It is a dish for special days such as Meskel, an Ethiopian feast day."},{"start":358.97,"end":363.31,"text":"In Ethiopia, raw beef is not a risk to avoid but a treat to celebrate."}],"words":[{"text":"The","start":0,"end":0.14},{"text":"first","start":0.14,"end":0.36},{"text":"surprising","start":0.36,"end":0.88},{"text":"dish","start":0.88,"end":1.2},{"text":"comes","start":1.2,"end":1.46},{"text":"from","start":1.46,"end":1.7},{"text":"Latin","start":1.7,"end":1.96},{"text":"America","start":1.96,"end":2.44},{"text":"Chapulines","start":3.72,"end":4.12},{"text":"are","start":4.12,"end":4.4},{"text":"grasshoppers","start":4.4,"end":5.14},